Falsifies `paging::sv39::the_two_halves_are_disjoint`.

The defect: the high half is decided by the sign bit, taken one position too low. Sv39 sign-
extends from bit 38, and "the high half is where the sign bit is set" is a correct reading of
the specification; getting the bit's number wrong by one is the mistake that reading invites,
because a 39-bit address has bits 38:0 and the count of bits and the index of the top bit differ
by one. With bit 37 standing in for it, every address with bit 37 set is in the kernel half,
including addresses that are squarely in the user half by the low test.

Expected to fail: `assert!(!(Sv39::in_half(Half::Low, va) && Sv39::in_half(Half::High, va)))`,
for any `va` with bit 37 set and bits 63:38 clear, `0x20_0000_0000` being the smallest.

**The half test is shared and the proof is not.** The `aarch64` record makes the low half an
inclusive range and the `x86_64` one turns the high test into a fits-in-the-mask test.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
@@ -488,7 +488,7 @@ pub trait PageFormat {
         let top = va >> Self::SPLIT_SHIFT;
         match half {
             Half::Low => top == 0,
-            Half::High => top == (u64::MAX >> Self::SPLIT_SHIFT),
+            Half::High => (va >> (Self::SPLIT_SHIFT - 1)) & 1 == 1,
         }
     }
 
